{
  "dom_id": "step-q351346",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/64b0f84b62adc0db3385a75d8fdc6e6707eb6778084b9e21de863ca6954c4c1d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=25$ and $x+5y=25$ for $y\\in [0,5]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=25.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+5y & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/f5b7cafc0ad6ab7624c1274451f16ba2c3c9a111ac9dad3abd90f082cc42d9b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/166dbb3f75000dfc155abfc3452ff6874dab1542409edc7e4867c5a5ee73c0b0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[\\,(25)-(25-5y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[5y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/9322b6f4bc9a0e913670f8dcbc9657206c3ae6a30de2f26308054d75b67d0861.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(25)-(25-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({(5y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(25{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{25{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{25(5)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(5)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3125}{3}-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
